PCI: dwc: do not warn about inbound iATU size when there are no dma-ranges

"PCI: dwc: devsoc: fix inbound ATU configuration" made dw_pcie_iatu_detect()
skip the inbound iATU probe when the host bridge node carries no dma-ranges
property, because in that case the PCIe-to-CPU mapping is 1:1 and neither the
detection nor the configuration of the inbound windows is needed.  As a result
num_ib_windows stays 0 on such platforms (e.g. ipq5332, whose DTS never uses
dma-ranges).

dw_pcie_iatu_setup() however checks the outcome of the (now possibly empty)
dma-ranges loop with a single statement:

	i = 0;
	resource_list_for_each_entry(entry, &pp->bridge->dma_ranges) {
		...
	}

	if (pci->num_ib_windows <= i)
		dev_warn(pci->dev, "Dma-ranges exceed inbound iATU size (%u)\n",
			 pci->num_ib_windows);

With an empty dma_ranges list the loop body never runs, so i is still 0 and
"0 <= 0" is true.  Every ipq5332 board therefore reports

  qcom-pcie 18000000.pcie: Dma-ranges exceed inbound iATU size (0)

at boot although it has no dma-ranges at all and the inbound window is
intentionally unused.  Apply the same condition that the detection uses and
only warn when there really are dma-ranges that did not fit into the available
inbound windows.

No functional change apart from the bogus log line.

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
@@ -723,8 +723,15 @@ static int dw_pcie_iatu_setup(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
 			return ret;
 		}
 	}
 
-	if (pci->num_ib_windows <= i)
+	/*
+	 * Do not complain when the node has no dma-ranges at all: the
+	 * PCIe-to-CPU mapping is 1:1 then and dw_pcie_iatu_detect() skipped
+	 * the inbound probe on purpose ("PCI: dwc: devsoc: fix inbound ATU
+	 * configuration"), so num_ib_windows is 0 and "0 <= 0" would warn
+	 * although there is nothing to configure.
+	 */
+	if (!list_empty(&pp->bridge->dma_ranges) && pci->num_ib_windows <= i)
 		dev_warn(pci->dev, "Dma-ranges exceed inbound iATU size (%u)\n",
 			 pci->num_ib_windows);
 
